# Provider credentials, by example. Copy to `.env` and fill in what you need.
#
# `.env` is gitignored and `.env.example` is not. Nothing here is a real key,
# and no real key belongs in any tracked file, in a design document, or in
# CLAUDE_LOG.md.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# YOU NEED NONE OF THESE TO RUN THE SUITE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every gate that runs on a pull request is deterministic:
#
#   Gate 1  pylint          static analysis
#   Gate 2  pytest -m unit  synthetic input, no network
#   Gate 3  pytest -m system replay mode
#   Gates 4-6                replay mode, recorded fixtures
#
# `--mode` defaults to `replay` precisely so an unconfigured clone cannot spend
# quota or emit an unmarked live result. **The whole suite passes with no key
# set**, which is the property worth stating; a count here only goes stale, and
# this one did.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT EACH KEY UNLOCKS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# GEMINI_API_KEY
#   The only key provisioned in v1 (A3). Serves as BOTH judge and candidate on
#   the free tier, which is why self-preference is recorded on every result
#   rather than corrected: the confound cannot be removed under this
#   configuration, and one that is recorded can be accounted for later.
#
#   Used by:  probe-model-version-nightly.yml   resolve the served model version
#             evaluate-live-weekly.yml          the gemini leg, live
#             diagnose-on-demand.yml            only when mode=live
#
#   Free tier at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
#
#   NOTE ON THE DAILY ALLOWANCE. The free tier permits 20 requests per day
#   per model, so recording a corpus is a multi-day activity and a run that
#   stops making progress is usually that rather than a fault. Measured
#   2026-09-26; see `running_jobs.md` section 6.2.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# GOOGLE_API_KEY
#   An ALTERNATIVE NAME, not a second credential. The Gen AI client resolves
#   either name for itself, so the gemini adapter declares this one too and
#   the orphan-credential check knows about both. Set one, not both.
GOOGLE_API_KEY=

# OPENAI_API_KEY
#   NOT provisioned in v1. The openai leg runs in replay, which needs no
#   credential. Adding a key later is a configuration change and not a refactor,
#   because --engine and --mode are orthogonal by design.
#
#   https://platform.openai.com/api-keys
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
#   NOT provisioned in v1, for the same reason. The claude leg runs in replay.
#
#   https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# XAI_API_KEY
#   NOT provisioned in v1. Grok speaks the OpenAI wire protocol, so the adapter
#   declares four values and inherits the protocol; adding the key is the whole
#   of enabling it (design section 3.3).
#
#   https://console.x.ai/
XAI_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IN CI
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Keys live in a GitHub Environment named `live`, never in repository secrets
# at large. Environment scoping makes the guarantee STRUCTURAL: a workflow that
# does not name the environment cannot reach the secret, so there is no
# conditional to get wrong.
#
# `gate-on-change.yml` names no environment and references no secret. It is the
# one workflow an untrusted party can trigger, and that is the whole of A1.
#
# `cmn/config.py` refuses to load any configuration file carrying a
# credential-shaped key, so a key cannot reach a tracked file by that route
# either.
